{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HoaKW7L5D7BpuTLWyG4Jqc","type":"tool_result","content":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-11-below-the-waterline.md:1308:- **D is wrong**, and stating why matters more than the right answer: nothing in Kubernetes installs drivers. This is the fourth instance of the pattern named at Ch 10 §3.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-08-standing-the-watch.md:713:[Omitted long matching line]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-08-standing-the-watch.md:1071:**§4 again.** The node controller observes heartbeats, compares them against what it expects, and acts: updating a condition, then evicting. It is a control loop. It is the sixth one in this book, and you could have predicted its structure without being told.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-09-every-pod-has-an-address.md:1204:**From §1.** And underneath all of it, none of the actual packet-moving is Kubernetes' work at all. Kubernetes defines the network model; a CNI plugin implements it [source: k8s-docs-extending-kubernetes-2026-08-23] — which is the second instance of an arrangement you first met in Chapter 2, where CRI does the same thing for container runtimes. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 2 §4 — CRI as the first pluggable interface]*\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-09-every-pod-has-an-address.md:1622:This is the second instance in the chapter of the same shape — a valid object whose effect depends on something that isn't there. Chapter 10 gives it a name.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-06-fleets-not-vessels.md:1465:And one thing that is not a bullet point: you have seen the control loop twice now, at two altitudes, and recognized it the second time. Hold onto that recognition. You are going to need it once more, and the third time is the one that matters."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0c6163c9-5ff8-483f-9ded-e88d1bb6eae7","uuid":"af36cdda-2aa9-43a9-b1e6-2e01c13969ac","timestamp":"2026-08-31T03:55:19.50